{
  "concept": "When two sides and a non-included angle (SSA) are known, the number of triangles that can close depends entirely on how the swinging side a compares with the altitude h = b·sin(α) and with the side b.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Row of four reference panels labeled (a), (b), (c), (d) beneath each sketch",
    "Panel (a) heading: 'No triangle, a < h'",
    "Panel (b) heading: 'Right triangle, a = h'",
    "Panel (c) heading: 'Two triangles, a > h, a < b'",
    "Panel (d) heading: 'One triangle, a ≥ b'",
    "Shared labeling in every panel: horizontal base from α on the left to β on the right",
    "Acute angle arc at α in every panel",
    "Vertex label α (lower left), β (lower right), γ (apex, above)",
    "Side b drawn as the solid ray from α up to γ, labeled b",
    "Side a drawn hanging from γ, labeled a (short vertical stub not reaching the base in (a); vertical to β in (b); two dashed segments in (c); sloping to β in (d))",
    "Small square right-angle marker at β in panel (b)",
    "Solid vertical altitude segment from γ to the base labeled h in panel (c)",
    "Visible gap between the end of side a and the base in panel (a)",
    "Large live construction panel: fixed base ray from α, fixed angle α arc, fixed side b to γ, dashed altitude h from γ with foot marker, and a circular arc of radius a centered at γ sweeping toward the base",
    "Intersection markers where the arc of radius a meets the base line (0, 1, or 2 hollow dots)",
    "Readout box showing current a, h = b·sin α, b, and the resulting count: 0, 1, or 2 triangles",
    "Highlight ring / colored outline on whichever of the four reference panels matches the current value of a",
    "Legend: solid black = given parts (α, b, base); red = swinging side a; blue dashed = altitude h"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "lengthA",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That sweeping the single unknown length a carries the construction continuously through zero, one, two, and back to one solution, with the transitions occurring exactly at a = h and a = b.",
      "label": "Length of side a (α = 35°, b = 5, h ≈ 2.87)",
      "range": [0.5, 7, 0.1],
      "default": 2.0
    },
    {
      "id": "showAltitude",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the altitude h = b·sin α is the shortest possible reach from γ to the base, so any a shorter than h simply cannot touch the base and any a equal to h touches it exactly once, perpendicularly.",
      "label": "Show altitude h and right-angle marker",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "id": "caseStep",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "That each of the four textbook panels is one snapshot of the same construction, tied to a specific inequality comparing a with h and b.",
      "label": "Step through the four cases",
      "states": ["a < h (no triangle)", "a = h (right triangle)", "h < a < b (two triangles)", "a ≥ b (one triangle)"],
      "default": "a < h (no triangle)"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The fixed given parts",
      "narration": "Angle α and side b are locked in place, so vertex γ never moves; only the length of side a is unknown. The dashed altitude h drops from γ perpendicular to the base and marks the shortest distance γ can ever reach.",
      "state": { "lengthA": 2.0, "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "a < h (no triangle)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Case (a): a is too short",
      "narration": "With a = 2.0 and h ≈ 2.87, the arc of radius a swung from γ never reaches the base — a visible gap remains, exactly as in panel (a). No triangle can close.",
      "state": { "lengthA": 2.0, "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "a < h (no triangle)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Case (b): a exactly equals h",
      "narration": "Grow a to 2.87 and the arc just grazes the base at a single point, landing perpendicularly at β. The right-angle square appears: exactly one triangle, and it is a right triangle.",
      "state": { "lengthA": 2.9, "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "a = h (right triangle)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Case (c): the ambiguous case",
      "narration": "For a between h and b, the arc crosses the base in two places, giving two different closing segments — the two dashed sides a of panel (c). Both are valid triangles with the same α, a, and b.",
      "state": { "lengthA": 4.0, "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "h < a < b (two triangles)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Case (d): a is long enough to overtake b",
      "narration": "Once a reaches or passes b, the second intersection falls on the wrong side of α — behind the vertex — so only one triangle survives. Turn the altitude off and the sketch is just the ordinary triangle of panel (d).",
      "state": { "lengthA": 5.5, "showAltitude": false, "caseStep": "a ≥ b (one triangle)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Side a is a compass arc swung from γ: whether it misses the base, touches it once, cuts it twice, or cuts it only once on the usable side is decided solely by comparing a with h = b·sin α and with b — that is the entire ambiguous SSA case.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Top band: the four original black-line panels in a horizontal row, all sharing the same labeling (horizontal base, arc at α on the left, β on the right, γ above, side b from α to γ, side a hanging from γ), each with its italic-math heading above and (a)–(d) centered below; panel (a) shows a short vertical a with a clear gap above the base, panel (b) shows a meeting the base at β with a small right-angle square, panel (c) shows a solid labeled altitude h plus two dashed segments each labeled a, panel (d) shows a broad triangle with a sloping down to β. Bottom (larger) area: one live construction at roughly twice the size — black base ray and black side b with the angle arc at α, blue dashed altitude h from γ with a small foot tick and right-angle square when a = h, a red arc of radius a centered at γ sweeping down toward the base, hollow red dots at each base intersection, and the resulting triangle(s) filled with faint red. A small readout in the corner prints a, h, b and 'triangles: 0 / 1 / 2'. The currently matching reference panel gets a soft yellow highlight box. Serif italic math labels (α, β, γ, a, b, h) throughout, matching the textbook typography.",
  "elementsNote": null
}

